-- Q3246: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 350-40-25, the three stages of internal-use software development are: (1) preliminary project stage (conceptual formulation, evaluating alternatives, determining needed technology), (2) application development stage (design, coding, installation, testing), and (3) post-implementation/operation stage (training, maintenance, minor upgrades). Costs are expensed in stages 1 and 3 and capitalized in stage 2.
Wrong (A): "Planning, coding, and testing" does not match the ASC 350-40 framework. Planning activities fall under the preliminary project stage, where costs are expensed, not capitalized.
Wrong (B): ASC 730 governs research and development costs, which use a different framework. Internal-use software follows ASC 350-40, not the R&D model.
Wrong (C): Project management lifecycle terminology (initiation, execution, closure) is not the accounting framework prescribed by ASC 350-40.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3246;

-- Q3247: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 350-40-25, costs incurred during the preliminary project stage--including evaluating vendor proposals, conducting feasibility studies, and selecting a platform--are expensed as incurred. Capitalization begins only when the project enters the application development stage and management commits to funding the project.
Wrong (B): Capitalizing preliminary stage costs as an intangible asset is not permitted. Only application development stage costs qualify for capitalization under ASC 350-40.
Wrong (C): Deferring costs until project completion has no basis in ASC 350-40. The standard requires immediate expensing of preliminary stage costs.
Wrong (D): There is no allocation method between capital and expense for preliminary stage costs. All such costs are expensed in full.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3247;

-- Q3249: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-40-25-9, post-implementation costs including training, maintenance, and minor modifications are expensed as incurred. These activities do not add significant new functionality to the software asset.
Wrong (A): Blanket three-year amortization does not apply to post-implementation costs. These costs are expensed immediately, not capitalized and amortized.
Wrong (C): Deferring costs until the next upgrade has no basis in ASC 350-40. Post-implementation costs are expensed in the period incurred.
Wrong (D): While ASC 350-40-25-10 permits capitalization of upgrades that add significant new functionality, routine maintenance, training, and minor modifications do not meet this threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3249;

-- Q3251: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-40-15-6A, a cloud computing hosting arrangement is classified as a service contract when the customer does not take possession of the software and the arrangement does not meet software license criteria. The key test is whether the customer can run the software independently or contract with another party to host it without significant penalty.
Wrong (A): Physical possession describes a software license arrangement, not a service contract. This choice describes the opposite classification.
Wrong (C): Contract length and renewal provisions are commercial terms that do not determine whether an arrangement is a license or service contract under ASC 350-40.
Wrong (D): The SaaS label alone does not determine classification. The key test is whether the customer can take possession of the software, regardless of how the vendor markets the arrangement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3251;

-- Q3252: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 350-40-35-1, capitalized internal-use software costs are amortized over the estimated useful life of the software, typically using the straight-line method unless another systematic method better represents the pattern of economic benefit. Amortization begins when the software is substantially ready for its intended use.
Wrong (B): The 40-year maximum amortization period applies to certain other intangible assets under ASC 350, not to internal-use software. Software typically has a much shorter useful life.
Wrong (C): Amortization begins when the software is ready for use, not upon disposal. Waiting until sale or replacement would overstate the asset''s carrying value.
Wrong (D): Amortization is based on the software''s estimated useful life, not a contractual license period. Internal-use software developed in-house may have no contractual period at all.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3252;

-- Q3253: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASU 2018-15 (codified in ASC 350-40), implementation costs for cloud computing service contracts follow the same capitalization framework as internal-use software. The $50,000 for vendor evaluation is a preliminary project stage cost and is expensed. The $200,000 for configuration and customization is an application development stage cost and is capitalized as a prepaid asset, then amortized over the service period.
Wrong (A): Expensing all $250,000 ignores the capitalization requirement for application development stage costs. Configuration and customization during implementation qualify for capitalization.
Wrong (B): Capitalizing all $250,000 improperly includes the $50,000 in preliminary stage costs, which must be expensed under ASC 350-40-25.
Wrong (D): This reverses the correct treatment. Vendor evaluation costs are expensed (preliminary stage), while configuration costs are capitalized (application development stage).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3253;

-- Q3257: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 350-40-25-7, training costs for internal-use software are always expensed as incurred, regardless of the development stage in which they occur. Training benefits users, not the software asset itself, and does not enhance the software''s functionality.
Wrong (B): Training costs cannot be capitalized as part of the software asset. Unlike coding and testing costs that directly contribute to the software''s functionality, training is an operating activity.
Wrong (C): Even during the application development stage, training costs are expensed. The capitalization rules for that stage apply only to activities like coding, testing, and data conversion.
Wrong (D): Deferring and amortizing training costs has no basis in ASC 350-40. Training is expensed immediately in the period incurred.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3257;

-- Q3258: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-40-35-1, when internal-use software is abandoned or management determines it will no longer be completed or used, the remaining unamortized capitalized costs must be written off immediately as a loss. An abandoned project provides no future economic benefit to justify continued capitalization.
Wrong (A): Continuing amortization over the original useful life is inappropriate because the asset will generate no future benefit. The carrying amount must be written off in the period of abandonment.
Wrong (B): Transferring abandoned software costs to goodwill has no basis in GAAP. Goodwill arises only from business combinations, not from internal project write-offs.
Wrong (D): Reclassifying to R&D expense is incorrect because internal-use software is governed by ASC 350-40, not ASC 730. Abandonment results in a loss, not an R&D reclassification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3258;

-- Q3261: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-40-25-2, testing during the application development stage is a necessary activity to ensure software functions as designed. These costs are capitalizable along with other development activities such as coding, installation, and data conversion.
Wrong (A): ASC 350-40 explicitly includes testing as a capitalizable development activity. It is not treated as a separate quality assurance function excluded from capitalization.
Wrong (B): Capitalization does not depend on test outcomes. Testing costs are capitalizable regardless of whether individual tests pass or fail during development.
Wrong (D): There is no one-month threshold for capitalizing testing costs under ASC 350-40. The duration of testing is irrelevant to capitalization eligibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3261;

-- Q3266: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Agile development uses iterative sprints that include activities from multiple ASC 350-40 stages simultaneously--planning (preliminary), coding and testing (application development), and user feedback (post-implementation). This blurs the clear stage boundaries that ASC 350-40 assumes, requiring careful tracking within each sprint to determine which costs qualify for capitalization.
Wrong (A): Agile projects can be capitalized under ASC 350-40. The entity must track costs by stage activity within each sprint to properly classify capitalizable versus expensed amounts.
Wrong (B): ASC 350-40 does not specify a required development methodology. The standard applies equally to waterfall, agile, and other approaches.
Wrong (C): The relative cost of agile versus waterfall development is irrelevant to the accounting challenge. The issue is stage boundary identification, not project expense levels.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3266;

